re Peng I have T1,T1, T6,T6, B5, F2, J2 \u2192 that\u2019s 2 pairs + 3 singles (needs 4 more pairs).  \nPeng T6 breaks the T6 pair for a meld, so not good for 7 pairs.  \n\nIf I aim for **standard 4 melds + 1 pair**:  \nCurrently: melds = Chow W4-W6, Pung T8, need 2 more melds.  \nHand before Peng: T1,T1,T6,T6,B5,F2,J2.  \nPossible melds: T1,T1 could be pair, T6,T6 could be pair or part of meld.  \nIf I Peng T6, I get 3rd meld, left T1,T1,B5,F2,J2 \u2192 only T1 pair, need 4th meld from B5,F2,J2 \u2014 impossible. So Peng leaves me far from ready.  \n\nBetter to **Pass**:  \n- Keep two pairs T1,T1 and T6,T6.  \n- Can aim for Seven Pairs by drawing more pairs from B5,F2,J2 singles.  \n- Or try to get Chow from B5 (like B4,B5,B6) or honor pung from F2/J2.  \n- Given prevailing wind South (F2), seat wind West (F3), F2 is not my seat wind but is prevailing wind, so F2 pung would give 2 points if I win.  \n- But drawing another F2 or J2 is possible.  \n\nPeng T6 uses up a pair and commits to a hard-to-complete hand. Passing keeps flexibility for Seven Pairs or mixed hand.  \n\n**Evaluation:** Passing is better than Peng T6 because Peng uses a pair for a meld and leaves hand with poor shape, while passing keeps two pairs and allows aiming for Seven Pairs or drawing into a better standard hand.  \n\n**Reason:** Peng T6 would leave only one pair in hand and three unrelated singles, making it very difficult to complete the last meld. Passing maintains more options, including Seven Pairs or completing melds without sacrificing a natural pair.  \n\n**Answer:** Pass"}
